{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791375335,"nonce":"/EIt89MhaM5GT4WBj2kNVDyP0w8=","message":"Implemented conservative byte-range load reuse in shared HIR. The proof follows constant single-index byte GEPs, scalar-pointer strides and word-preserving pointer/integer casts. Accesses must share one SSA root and have nonzero, non-overlapping byte intervals whose endpoints fit a 32-bit address space. The address index is built lazily; lookups are memoized and depth bounded. No struct/array layout, multiple-index GEP, dynamic offset, pointer-sized stride, loaded-pointer identity or phi identity is inferred. Distinct roots remain potentially aliasing.\n\nLoad type identity and volatile barriers are preserved. Aggregate values carried as pointers cannot acquire an incorrectly small scalar write extent. Calls, atomics, fences, vector writes and unsupported/overlapping accesses remain barriers. No frontend, ABI, tier threshold, fast-math or Wasmtime changes.\n\nLate box expansion is followed by load CSE. Final removal of dead predecessors is followed by phi/CFG/value/load cleanup. Aggregate splitting then removes newly exposed whole-struct read/copy/extract sequences. The attempted restriction to functions with immediate memory savings did not recover the fannkuch between-build gap and was discarded; it is not part of the implementation.\n\nTwo safe diagnostic controls are available: ZYNTAX_DISABLE_LOAD_RANGES=1 disables range retention; ZYNTAX_DISABLE_LATE_LOAD_CLEANUP=1 disables the newly added late cleanup. Runtime switches do not undo build-time builtins snapshots. The broader Cranelift alias-region mapping remains open in 02563f8f234c45ce68f81f5479e139cb89be51fa68b0d9d7d74c3b01378d324f; unclassified Cranelift loads cannot simply be assumed to track classified stores.\n\nFinal Mac ARM64 native inspection, both controls off/on in the same executable: Lua NBody.advance HIR falls from 126 to 114 loads and 110 to 77 blocks; energy remains -169096. Artifacts /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-v9-{off,on}{.ir,.out,-hir}. These are code-inspection results, not Mac timing claims.\n\nFinal NUC measurement: LLVM disabled, interpreter-first/Cranelift tiering, CPU affinity 0-7, shared NUC lock held. Baseline is saved 5feb4e57ec30e464b0547cad10a2f0c357d64d75. \"Off\" disables both new runtime transforms in the candidate; \"on\" enables both. Order rotates across the three arms. Lua medians use 15 independent processes per arm. Python fannkuch uses five processes per arm with harness count 50. Wall timing excludes binary-hash metadata collection. Existing desktop/HUD processes remained running.\n\n| Kernel | Arm | Kernel/mean ms | Process wall ms |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| lua nbody | baseline | 132.761 | 208.389 |\n| lua nbody | off | 134.864 | 212.799 |\n| lua nbody | on | 128.917 | 204.562 |\n| lua tokenize | baseline | 146.677 | 183.151 |\n| lua tokenize | off | 149.529 | 187.586 |\n| lua tokenize | on | 149.708 | 185.264 |\n| python fannkuch | baseline | 39.403 | 2032.657 |\n| python fannkuch | off | 41.884 | 2157.544 |\n| python fannkuch | on | 41.829 | 2154.893 |\n\nLua nbody improves 2.9% kernel and 1.8% wall versus the saved baseline; the same-executable off/on comparison improves 4.4% kernel and 3.9% wall. This is a modest, repeatable nbody gain, not an all-kernel speedup. Tokenizer remains about 2% slower in kernel time versus the saved baseline, while its same-executable control is essentially flat.\n\nFannkuch remains about 6% slower versus the saved executable, both with the new runtime transforms disabled and enabled. All 77 traced functions in the disabled candidate match baseline native instruction sequences after normalizing embedded addresses. The enabled candidate changes four frontend bodies by removing jump blocks, with essentially unchanged timing versus disabled. The cause of the remaining between-build execution gap is unproven. Tracked separately, still open: 37a5c7b2b06f137b3acf9e22490fb1ba4baa2e5d28b6b9bc4940f6860fff9924. No broad Python improvement or new PyPy/LuaJIT win is claimed.\n\nArtifacts on Mac and NUC: /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-v9-control-final.json and its driver; /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-fannkuch-{baseline,v9off,v9on}-all.trace. JSON records binary hashes and individual samples. The earlier v6 all-kernel sweep, v7/v8 confirmations and range-only controls are retained as /tmp/zyntax-nuc-load-ranges-*.json; they are intermediate candidates, not final-binary claims. Initial v9 wall results included metadata hashing and are superseded by the final driver.\n\nFinal source SHA-256 values (local and NUC match):\nbf527a996ca0695f4f7115990666ffce78fa0deaeac49c7a7653fdfef000e060  crates/compiler/src/lib.rs\nc6c25490709187bbf2e22f56a7a30176ee1a5dcb3294e54616187c87e2a0a0f1  crates/compiler/src/load_cse.rs\nc197f8694d9d574e59836511c2028b1929b8f6fb4742870bf90a7305142f590d  crates/compiler/tests/load_ranges.rs\n\nFinal validation passed on Mac ARM64 and NUC x86-64:\n- Seven load-CSE unit tests, including byte-range overlap enumeration and declared aggregate-load types.\n- Six integration tests: original/optimized execution through interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM; same-object fields, partial overlap, identical addresses, distinct parameter roots that alias, negative offsets, scalar strides, cast chains, volatile and different-type loads, aggregate carrier widths, unsupported array/multi-index layouts, and dead-predecessor cleanup. Unaligned overlap cases run through the native backends only because the interpreter's raw scalar reads require natural alignment.\n- 43 Lua and 50 Python conformance tests, once with LLVM disabled and again with LLVM enabled, on both machines. Three official Lua suites were explicitly excluded.\n- NUC: all 14 OSR transfer and three loop-specialization regressions.\n- Final candidate passes all 17 Lua and 22 supported Python benchmark checksums. pidigits remains unsupported. Artifact /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-final-checks-v9.json locally, /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-final-checks.json on NUC; final log /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-final-checks-v9.log on NUC.\n- Formatting and staged diff checks passed. Only the three compiler files in this change are staged; the user's four untracked files are preserved.\n\nFinal test logs use /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-*-v9*.log locally and /tmp/zyntax-nuc-load-ranges-*-v9*.log on NUC.","files":null}]}